UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 26, Lesson 1: Days of national and local significance', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Days of national and local significance are times when communities remember or celebrate something important. National examples include Anzac Day on 25 April, Remembrance Day on 11 November, National Sorry Day on 26 May and NAIDOC Week in July. Local examples include town anniversaries and local memorial days.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort each day into remember, celebrate or both: Anzac Day; a town anniversary picnic; NAIDOC Week.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAnzac Day is mainly remember. A town anniversary picnic is mainly celebrate. NAIDOC Week celebrates First Nations culture, though it also honours history, so it can be both.\n\nWATCH FOR\nTreating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a calendar of six days of significance, including one local day found from a council or library source. For each record the date, what it marks, why it matters and how it is observed. Name your sources.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Days of national and local significance are times when communities remember or celebrate something important. National examples include Anzac Day on 25 April, Remembrance Day on 11 November, National Sorry Day on 26 May and NAIDOC Week in July. Local examples include town anniversaries and local memorial days.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a calendar of six days of significance, including one local day found from a council or library source. For each record the date, what it marks, why it matters and how it is observed. Name your sources. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Anzac Day is mainly remember. A town anniversary picnic is mainly celebrate. NAIDOC Week celebrates First Nations culture, though it also honours history, so it can be both. Reasoning: I used commemorate by attending to significance and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Treating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting. Correction: Anzac Day is mainly remember. A town anniversary picnic is mainly celebrate. NAIDOC Week celebrates First Nations culture, though it also honours history, so… Why: the correction restores accurate use of commemorate.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Treating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating commemorate on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with commemorate, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Anzac Day is mainly remember. A town anniversary picnic is mainly celebrate. NAIDOC Week celebrates First Nations culture, though it also honours history, so… This matches the lesson skill for commemorate.","options":["Anzac Day is mainly remember. A town anniversary picnic is mainly celebrate. NAIDOC Week celebrates First Nations culture, though it also honours history, so…","Treating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Sort each day into remember, celebrate or both: Anzac Day; a town anniversary picnic; NAIDOC Week. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Treating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of significance and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Treating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting.","Days of national and local significance are times when communities remember or celebrate something important. National examples include Anzac Day on 25 April,…","Anzac Day is mainly remember. A town anniversary picnic is mainly celebrate. NAIDOC Week celebrates First Nations culture, though it also honours history, so…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising commemorate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named significance and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the significance and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising commemorate. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Anzac Day is mainly remember. A town anniversary picnic is mainly celebrate. NAIDOC Week celebrates First Nations culture, though it also honours history, so… uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Anzac Day is mainly remember. A town anniversary picnic is mainly celebrate. NAIDOC Week celebrates First Nations culture, though it also honours history, so…","Treating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about commemorate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Treating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about commemorate.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about significance and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example calendar entries: 25 April, Anzac Day, remember people who served in war and operations; 26 May, National Sorry Day, remember…","Treating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Days of national and local significance are times when communities remember or celebrate something… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Treating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting.","Make a calendar of six days of significance, including one local day found from a council or library source. For each record the date, what it…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of commemorate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating commemorate on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Anzac Day is mainly remember. A town anniversary picnic is mainly celebrate. NAIDOC Week celebrates First Nations culture, though it also honours history, so it can be both. Reasoning: I used commemorate by attending to significance and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with commemorate, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Treating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting. Correction: Anzac Day is mainly remember. A town anniversary picnic is mainly celebrate. NAIDOC Week celebrates First Nations culture, though it also honours history, so… Why: the correction restores accurate use of commemorate.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w26-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise commemorate.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use commemorate with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify significance and explain why it matters.","I can practise date independently on a new prompt.","I can notice celebrate needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Days of national and local significance are times when communities remember or celebrate something important. National examples include Anzac Day on 25 April, Remembrance Day on 11 November, National Sorry Day on 26 May and NAIDOC Week in July. Local examples include town…","options":["Days of national and local significance are times when communities remember or celebrate something important. National examples include Anzac Day on 25 April, Remembrance Day on 11 November, National Sorry Day on 26 May and NAIDOC Week in July. Local examples include town anniversaries and local memorial days.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about commemorate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample calendar entries: 25 April, Anzac Day, remember people who served in war and operations; 26 May, National Sorry Day, remember Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children taken from their families; first week in July, NAIDOC Week, celebrate First Nations history and culture. Each entry has a date, purpose and way the day is marked.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort each day into remember, celebrate or both: Anzac Day; a town anniversary picnic; NAIDOC Week.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAnzac Day is mainly remember. A town anniversary picnic is mainly celebrate. NAIDOC Week celebrates First Nations culture, though it also honours history, so it can be both.","explain":"Days of national and local significance are times when communities remember or celebrate something important. National examples include Anzac Day on 25 April, Remembrance Day on 11 November, National Sorry Day on 26 May and NAIDOC Week in July. Local examples include town anniversaries and local memorial days.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Treating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"National days of remembrance.","caption":"Date, meaning, how marked.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">25 April</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Anzac Day, remember service</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">26 May</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Sorry Day, remember children</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example calendar entries: 25 April, Anzac Day, remember people who served in war and operations; 26 May, National Sorry  Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example calendar entries: 25 April, Anzac Day, remember people who served in war and operations; 26 May, National Sorry  Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about commemorate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample calendar entries: 25 April, Anzac Day, remember people who served in war and operations; 26 May, National Sorry Day, remember Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children taken from their families; first week in July, NAIDOC Week, celebrate First Nations history and culture. Each entry has a date, purpose and way the day is marked. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Celebrate, remember or both.","caption":"Add one local day.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">July</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">NAIDOC Week, celebrate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Local</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">town anniversary</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about commemorate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTreating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting. Here is a more productive model: Example calendar entries: 25 April, Anzac Day, remember people who served in war and operations; 26 May, National Sorry Day, remember Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children taken from their families; first week in July, NAIDOC Week, celebrate First Nations history and culture. Each entry has a date, purpose and way the day is marked.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAnzac Day is mainly remember. A town anniversary picnic is mainly celebrate. NAIDOC Week celebrates First Nations culture, though it also honours history, so it can be both.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Treating every special day as a holiday for fun leaves out days that are mainly for remembering and reflecting. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about commemorate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDays of national and local significance are times when communities remember or celebrate something important. National examples include Anzac Day on 25 April, Remembrance Day on 11 November, National Sorry Day on 26 May and NAIDOC Week in July. Local examples include town anniversaries and local memorial days. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 26, Lesson 1: Days of national and local significance","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Community and remembrance","week":26,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample calendar entries: 25 April, Anzac Day, remember people who served in war and operations; 26 May, National Sorry Day, remember Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children taken from their families; first week in July, NAIDOC Week, celebrate First Nations history and culture. Each entry has a date, purpose and way the day is marked.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","wo